== differences from aristotelian logic ==
[[aristotelian_logic]] was fundamentally a term based logic

=== predicates vs subjects ===
variables in subject position are exposed to quantification.
No Frege was always very happy to quantify over predicates(/concepts/functions)

Indeed, in any well-formed syllogism (even invalid ones), at least one term must
occur in subject position in one statement and in predicate position in another.

Names, phrases or variables in the place of subjects (the terms to which
predicates were then applied) can never occur as predicates. Instead of being
predicated of each other, Frege's logic only allowed categorical terms to be
predicated of objects not of each other.

According to Aristotelian logic, the statement “Every smartphone is electronic”
is about the relationship between two properties or traits.

The term “electronic” is predicated of the subject “smartphone”, and the
sentence as a whole states that there is some kind of connection between these
two properties.
In Frege's system, by contrast, both these terms occur as predicates.


On Frege's analysis, this statement
was to be rendered as “Each thing that is a smartphone is electronic” and
was true just in case

Instead of reporting some connection between the two traits, this statement
states something about the objects possessing them, namely that every object
that possesses the property of being a smartphone also possess the property of
being electronic. On Frege's reading 
and it involved not one predicate but two.


not a relationship between the qualities these terms signified
but
that 
the subject of this sentence are the objects of which those predicates are true

Instead of taking “electronic” to be true of the quality signified by
“smartphones”, “electronic” was true

According to Frege
“Every positive integer can be represented as the sum of four squares.”
“The number 20 can be represented as the sum of four squares”

but it was when variables were put into place

The same variable could appear in multiple places in a statement, but it could
not appear as both a predicate and a subject.

that variable could occur in other places through the sentence, allowing cross
reference in ways that Aristotelian logic did not allow for.


Not only did this bring out the connection with the inferential connections
connected with terms such as “and”, “or” and “not”

this allowed cross reference

but it was absolutley unacceptable to have the a variable occur in both subject
and predicate position.

=== is ===
Freges zealous distinction between objects (things that are denoted by terms
occurring as subjects) and concepts (things denoted by terms occurring as
predicates) is also intimately connected with his distinction between the use of
'is' as a copula in predication and its use to express identity (or existence as
in “Socrates is”)
    Surely one can just as well assert of a thing that it is Alexander the Great,
    or is the number four, or is the planet Venus, as that it is green or is a
    mammal? If anybody thinks this, he is not distinguishing the usages
    of the word 'is'. In the last two examples it serves as a copula, as a mere
    verbal sign of predication. ...  We are here saying that something falls
    under a concept, and the grammatical predicate stands for this concept. In
    the first three examples, on the other hand, 'is' is used like the 'equals'
    sign in arithmetic, to express an equation. ... An equation is reversible;
    an object's falling under a concept is an irreversible relation.
											[[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]

Identity permitted substitution just as the mathematicians used to such good
effect in finding values to satisfy their equations

In [[aristotelian_logic#categorical statements]] categorematic terms

