=== summary ===

== courses of values ==
Frege does not introduce sets not use the '∈' symbol in his notation.
In place of sets he uses courses of values (werthverläufe, singular:
werthverlauf - “die werthverläufe von Funktionen”). The course of values of the
predicate P is ε̂P(ε) (the values of ε for which P(ε)

Even here he never relies on any claim that an object is or is not a member of a
course of values. Indeed he simply applies the predicate to a term denoting it.
i.e. Frege's formal work never contains a statement such as
        x ∊ ε̂P(ε)
wherever such a statement might be used Frege simply uses:
        P(x)

However, these courses of values are among the objects in the domain of
quantification of first(? zeroth?) order objects. Like trains and atoms, courses
of values were things of which concepts can be predicated.

*basic law V* in [[local:zz.texts/frege1893-grundgesetzeDerArithmetic.ebertTrans.pdf|grundgesetze]]
    ε̂f(ε) = α̂g(α) ↔ ∀x(f(x) IFF  g(x))
(Although this rendering in modern notation is close, Frege's concept script
does not distinguish between concepts and functions.)

I think this implies that each function(/predicate/concept) has a matching
course of values:
      ε̂f(ε) = α̂g(α) ↔  ∀x(f(x) = g(x))
    ∴ ε̂f(ε) = α̂f(α) ↔  ∀x(f(x) = f(x))
    ∴ ∃z( z = α̂f(α) ↔  ∀x(f(x) = f(x)) )
    but ∀x(f(x) = f(x)) is a theorem
    ∴ ∃z( z = α̂f(α) )

This also allows the definition of a membership operator within Frege's notation:
    x ∊ y =df. ∃f( y = ε̂f(ε) ∧ f(x) )

Which give rise to:
=== Russells paradox ===

==== solutions ====
All methods of avoiding the paradox amount to circumscribing this range of
sets(/courses of values).

Either by constraining the range of concepts/functions/open sentences (so that
some statements such as “This statement is false” cannot be expressed in the
notation at all) or by admitting such statements but constraining the range of
courses of values/extensions so that some concepts/functions/open sentences do
not have a corresponding extension/course of values.

Russell's *type theory* solution takes the first option by limiting the range

==== basic law V′ ====
In response to Russell's paradox (which Frege first heard of while Vol.2 of
grundgesetze was going to the press) Frege suggested a modification to
basic law V (in the appendix to Vol.2 of [[local:zz.texts/frege1893-grundgesetzeDerArithmetic.ebertTrans.pdf|grundgesetze]]).
    V′)		ε̂f(ε) = α̂g(α) ↔  ∀x(f(x) = g(x))

This solution does not work. See [[local:zz.texts/quine1955-fregesWayOut.pdf]] for
a formal proof. This paper primarily of interest in tracing the connection
from Frege to Quine, and in making clear Quine's view of Russell's use of attributes
and classes in _Principia_.
